// @test: run
// @tier: 0,1
// @level: L0
// @tags: newtype, reference, cbgr, differential, regression
// @timeout: 60000
// @expected-exit: 0
//
// A TRANSPARENT WRAPPER REACHED THROUGH A REFERENCE UNWRAPS TO ITS VALUE
// (T1438).
//
// A transparent wrapper has no runtime identity: its register holds the
// inner value, so `x.0` is genuinely free and the codegen emits an
// identity `Mov` for it. That is right, and it stops being right the
// moment the register holds a REFERENCE to the wrapper instead — the
// same `Mov` then hands back the ADDRESS.
//
// The two cases were indistinguishable at the decision point because
// `infer_expr_type_name` erases the `&`: both `NT` and `&NT` answer "NT",
// so both took the branch. Measured before the fix:
//
//     fn unwrap_by_ref(p: &NT) -> Int { p.0 }
//     unwrap_by_ref(&NT(42))     Tier 0: 42      Tier 1: 6134571104
//
// The number is a stack address, which is why this is a spec and not a
// crash report: nothing refuses, nothing warns, and the programme
// continues with a plausible-looking integer. Downstream that became
// `File.write` returning Err at Tier 1 — `write()` was handed a file
// descriptor in the billions and answered EBADF.
//
// RUN AT BOTH TIERS, because the interpreter was already right and a
// regression that breaks it instead would otherwise read as success.
// Tier 0 encodes a reference as a register reference and Tier 1 as the
// address of an alloca; the fix reads through either.
//
// THE VALUE FORMS ARE HERE TOO, and they are the load-bearing half: a
// fix that simply stopped treating wrappers as transparent would pass
// every reference case above and quietly make `NT(42).0` cost a heap
// read. A named-field record sits beside them for the same reason — it
// never took the transparent path and must not start.

type NT is (Int);
type Rec is { a: Int };

fn unwrap_by_ref(p: &NT) -> Int { p.0 }
fn unwrap_by_mut(p: &mut NT) -> Int { p.0 }
fn unwrap_by_value(p: NT) -> Int { p.0 }
fn record_by_ref(p: &Rec) -> Int { p.a }

fn main() {
    let mut n = NT(42);
    let r = Rec { a: 77 };

    // PRINT BEFORE ASSERTING: `assert_eq` says two values differ and
    // never says what they were, and "returned the address" and
    // "returned the wrong field" are different defects.
    let direct = n.0;
    print(f"direct={direct}");
    assert(direct == 42);

    let by_value = unwrap_by_value(n);
    print(f"by_value={by_value}");
    assert(by_value == 42);

    // THE SUBJECT — both reference forms.
    let by_ref = unwrap_by_ref(&n);
    print(f"by_ref={by_ref}");
    assert(by_ref == 42);

    let by_mut = unwrap_by_mut(&mut n);
    print(f"by_mut={by_mut}");
    assert(by_mut == 42);

    // A named-field record through a reference never used the
    // transparent path and must still not.
    let rec = record_by_ref(&r);
    print(f"record={rec}");
    assert(rec == 77);

    print("a wrapper through a reference unwraps to its value");
}
